  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "release.sh",
      "content": "This `|| die` never fires when `source` fails. Bash command substitutions don't inherit errexit unless `inherit_errexit` is set, and the `||` context turns errexit off anyway. So `printf` still runs and the subshell exits 0. If release.env has a syntax error or a failing line before `SILENT_EARLY_USERS=`, the result is an empty list. That passes `check_early_users` because blank is allowed, and the release goes out silent with no early users, with no message. The header promises the script stops in this case. Make the failure explicit.",
      "suggestion_code": "    source \"$signing_env\" || exit 1\n    printf '%s' \"${SILENT_EARLY_USERS:-}\"\n  )\" || die \"could not read SILENT_EARLY_USERS from $signing_env\"",
      "existing_code": "    source \"$signing_env\"\n    printf '%s' \"${SILENT_EARLY_USERS:-}\"\n  )\" || die \"could not read SILENT_EARLY_USERS from $signing_env\"",
      "start_line": 140,
      "end_line": 142,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/kevbox/release/SilentUpdater.kt",
      "content": "The code handles a release with no `kevbox-silent.json` asset the same way as a failed fetch of an asset that exists (timeout, `IOException`, non-2xx such as a GitHub CDN 5xx or 429). In both cases `seen(..., hasSilent = false)` replaces a good earlier record, and `fallbackNeeded` then returns true straight away. One network blip during a check makes the Core prompt card show on the next launch, on every Android 12+ box that was waiting for the rollout. Only a missing asset (`silentUrl == null`) and a body that fails to parse should count as \"no block\". On a fetch error, return something like `\"silent-fetch-failed\"` and leave the stored record alone.",
      "suggestion_code": "        val block = when (val url = release.silentUrl) {\n            null -\u003e null\n            else -\u003e when (val fetched = fetchBlock(url)) {\n                BlockFetch.Failed -\u003e return \"silent-fetch-failed\" // transient: keep the previous record\n                is BlockFetch.Done -\u003e fetched.block // null when the file does not parse\n            }\n        }\n        prefs.seen(release.version, hasSilent = block != null, afterBoxMs = block?.let { it.afterMs + (now() - serverNow) })",
      "existing_code": "        val block = release.silentUrl?.let { fetchBlock(it) }\n        // `after` stored on the box's own clock (moved by the box's offset from the server), so the\n        // 72-hour net, which reads the box clock, works on a box whose date is wrong.\n        prefs.seen(release.version, hasSilent = block != null, afterBoxMs = block?.let { it.afterMs + (now() - serverNow) })",
      "start_line": 130,
      "end_line": 133,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/kevbox/release/SilentUpdatePrefs.kt",
      "content": "`afterBoxMs` is worked out as `afterMs + (now() - serverNow)`. `serverNow` comes from the `Date` header, which only has one-second precision, while `now()` is in milliseconds, so the result changes by up to about a second on every check. This equality almost never holds, and the prefs are rewritten on every check. Comparing with a tolerance (for example, within a few seconds) would keep the skip-write guard working.",
      "existing_code": "            seenAfterBoxMs() == afterBoxMs \u0026\u0026",
      "start_line": 48,
      "end_line": 48,
      "category": "performance",
      "severity": "low"
    }
  ],
